          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with painless bright-red bleeding that drips into the toilet </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with painless bright-red bleeding that drips into the toilet bowl after defaecation. Based on the dentate line rule, where does this lesion most likely originate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Below the dentate line</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Below the dentate line is innervated by somatic branches of the inferior rectal nerve, making lesions there painful. Painless bleeding points above the line, where autonomic nerves carry no pain.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Above the dentate line</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Above the dentate line, autonomic innervation means no pain sensation. Internal haemorrhoids arise here and bleed painlessly with bright-red blood dripping into the pan.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>At the anal verge</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The anal verge lies below the dentate line and is supplied by somatic nerves, making it a painful zone. A thrombosed external haemorrhoid here would cause severe pain, not painless bleeding.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>In the ischiorectal fossa</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The ischiorectal fossa is a perianal space where abscesses form, presenting with constant throbbing pain, swelling, and fever — not painless bleeding.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Above the dentate line, autonomic innervation carries no pain fibres — internal haemorrhoids bleed painlessly. Below the line, somatic nerve supply of the inferior rectal nerve makes any lesion exquisitely painful."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The dentate line rule: autonomic nerves above make internal haemorrhoids painless; somatic nerves below make fissures and thrombosed external haemorrhoids agonising."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
