          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An anal fissure located laterally, away from the posterior midline, should raise clinical suspicion for which underlying condition?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pilonidal sinus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A pilonidal sinus is a hair-containing sinus of the natal cleft — an entirely separate condition unrelated to fissure position. Lateral fissures point to systemic inflammatory or infective causes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Internal haemorrhoids</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemorrhoids are enlargements of vascular anal cushions, not mucosal tears. They do not cause or alter the location of anal fissures.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Crohn's disease</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Typical fissures form posteriorly because blood supply is poorest and mechanical stress greatest there. A lateral or atypical fissure is a red flag for Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, malignancy, or HIV.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rectal prolapse</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rectal prolapse involves protrusion of rectal wall layers through the anus. It does not cause mucosal tears and is unrelated to fissure location.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Typical anal fissures almost always occur at the posterior midline due to poor blood supply. A fissure in any other location — particularly lateral — should prompt investigation for Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, malignancy, or HIV."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "A posterior midline fissure is typical and benign. Any lateral or atypical fissure warrants investigation for Crohn's disease, tuberculosis, malignancy, or HIV."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
