          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with a chronic anal fissure has not responded to stool softeners and t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with a chronic anal fissure has not responded to stool softeners and topical glyceryl trinitrate after six weeks. What is the next appropriate step in management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemorrhoidectomy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemorrhoidectomy is an operation for haemorrhoids, not fissures. The fissure management ladder escalates from topical therapy to botulinum toxin before any sphincter surgery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rubber-band ligation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rubber-band ligation is used for grade I–II internal haemorrhoids in clinic. It has no role in the management of anal fissures.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediate lateral internal sphincterotomy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lateral internal sphincterotomy is the definitive surgical option but is reserved for truly refractory fissures. Botulinum toxin is attempted first to avoid permanent sphincter division.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Botulinum toxin injection</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The fissure ladder is: stool softeners → topical GTN or diltiazem → botulinum toxin → lateral internal sphincterotomy. Each step treats proportionately while protecting sphincter function.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "After topical GTN or diltiazem fails, the next step is botulinum toxin injection into the internal sphincter. Lateral internal sphincterotomy is reserved for truly refractory cases because it permanently divides part of the sphincter."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The fissure management ladder treats proportionately: stool softeners → topical GTN/diltiazem → botulinum toxin → lateral internal sphincterotomy only as a last resort."
                }
              ],
